s and yet nearly 80 percent of all  college athletic directors are men what  do you think is key to hiring more  female coaches well I think people hire  people who look like them and you have a  lot of white men in athletics especially  as the athletic director and they're  gonna hire a lot of men because they're  comfortable with that they're gonna look  at the guys on the men's side and say  hey why don't you come over and coach on  the women's side  we never do the reverse and I think it's  just so important and the way women go  for jobs we need to be more aggressive  but what did your passion come from  though that day because were you  intending to say that at that particular  time in that particular place you know I  was not intending to say that I don't my  friends ago where did that come from but  you know we had just come through Black  History Month then we had Women's  History Month and I saw someone say why  don't we have men's History Month and I  said because men's history is taught in  school we learn about men in the  curriculum we know all about what men  did when I think back what woman was  famous in history when we were in school  and Betsy Ross you know and she was  famous for sewing so where are all these  great accomplished women yeah but yeah  but I heard people say why did she have  to say it why don't you just do it why  was it important for you to say it  because people could say now you're  discriminating against men you know I  and I did do it about I've had an  all-female staff probably for the past  eight years I have had men on myself and  I do hire men I have a video coordinator  and a sports information I do hire men  but on my staff I think it's important  for the women that we're coaching to  look up to that staff and see that and  it's just we've been so successful we  have great chemistry I think it's just a  great situation I wish when my former  players came out looking for jobs I wish  more women we're able to get those jobs  but they're just not out there how much  of this is a message that you convey to  the young women that you coach they get  that message all the time any time a  prominent woman comes on campus we had  kind of Lisa Rice come by a couple of  weeks ago we have them come over and  talk to the team because I want them to  see these successful women there really  aren't that many of them I want to talk  about the numbers because this was  stunning to me I touched on title nine  when it was enacted in 1972 ninety  percent of the coaches of female  basketball teams were women now it's  down to 41 percent  I thought title nine was supposed to  guarantee equality in education and  sports wife the number decreasing well  that was the idea and when it came out  of course it took 30 years probably for  us to get to any kind of equality when  I'm not sure was still there what  happened was when we got to the point in  the 90s they started paying more that's  when the men came in suddenly the salary  was worth their time coming over from  the men's side they couldn't break into  the men's game here's an opportunity on  the women's side to make some money  we've looked at the u.s. women's  national soccer team we've covered that  story for the first time now all of them  are going to sue for equal pay  are we the transformational moment do  you think with women in sports well I  really hope so we thought maybe when  Billie Jean King started that years ago  that we things would change and now the  women's soccer team is stepping forward  and making a big push and maybe we'll  see something from the WNBA what still  needs to be done Lots needs to be done I  think first it starts with hiring women  and I think if girls aren't looking up  and seeing these role models how are  they gonna learn what it's supposed to  be like and how are they gonna believe  that they can do it and if they can't  believe it how are we gonna change it  Budaj is your mayor he said you are a  leader that he admires what do you think  about mayor Buda jizz I think he's  awesome I was down there for his  announcement on Sunday he's somebody  that is a great voice and he's got a  different perspective than we've had  he's young he knows this generation and  I think he's the future can I just say  you're the first Buffett I've ever met  what is the name on your birth  certificate it was not Muffet but it is  on my driver's license now it's my legal  name you don't want to tell me what it  is well it is an my mom's name was Ann  nobody cuz here's Muffet her husband  Matt is here and they have a son Murphy  I'm thinking him as a great letter at  your house  congratulations on being on thank you 48  women top 48 women that means a lot  coming back
 

